  "id": "pashto_landay_tappa",
  "name": "Tappa and landay (Pashto folk couplet song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Folk song of the Pashtuns of southern and eastern Afghanistan and the Pakistani frontier, built on the landay or tappa, an anonymous couplet of nine and thirteen syllables sung in long strings of verses, alongside longer stanzaic forms such as the charbeta. Widely broadcast by Radio Afghanistan and Peshawar radio, it is sung by soloists or alternating singers with rubab, harmonium and drum, and by women among themselves at home and at weddings.",
  "recipe": "classic modern tappa and landay, ballad Poetry, med east poetic branch. many notes per syllable, solo with instrumental accompaniment. rhythmic strum percussive dance driving rubab, vocal male song accompaniment hand pumped harmonium, tabla. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
